ng those four flags, but the prerequisite modules haven't been created yet. Since I can't proceed without them, I'm going to create the minimal set of dependencies—the sys package with file stat operations, testutils for differential testing, and the format package for column handling—then build out the full ls command with all the required flag implementations and their corresponding tests. column output), I can focus on the core functionality without worrying about complex formatting logic. Let me start with the essential files: go.mod for dependencies, the sys package with platform-specific implementations for file stats and terminal handling, testutils for the differential testing framework, and finally the main ls command with its test suite.\n\nFor the acceptance criteria, I need to support single-column output with inode and block count prefixes, long format with numeric UID/GID, type indicators, and human-readable sizes, plus color support that at least doesn't break anything.\n\nI also need to handle dotfile filtering, directory-only listing, basic C locale sorting, and a total line for long format. Let me check if gls is available for differential testing, though since pkg/testutils doesn't exist, I'll need to focus on getting the code to compile with the required flags. The module is github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils and I can use golang.org/x/sys for syscall abstractions.\n\nNow I'm setting up the project structure with go.mod, minimal implementations of pkg/sys and pkg/testutils, the main ls command, and its tests. Let me determine if this is a Darwin or Linux system first.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1211,"cache_read_input_tokens":92561,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1211},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f938a